  <data name="Home_IdeaBody" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Dependencies, metrics and risk come from the real T-SQL syntax tree, produced by Microsoft's official parser: they are exact and reproducible, and there is no way for the model to invent a table that does not exist. The language model only does what a parser never could — interpret business intent and propose a design — and it does so already fed with those verified facts.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_IdeaClaim" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>What can be known for certain is computed. What requires judgement is asked of the model.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_IdeaTitle" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The idea behind the project</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_Lead" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>From a legacy SQL Server script to documentation and a migration plan.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_Problem" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>In many companies the real business logic does not live in the application code, but buried in stored procedures written years ago by people who have long since left. With no documentation. The first wall in any modernisation is not technical but a matter of knowledge: nobody knows what that code does, or where to start without breaking things.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_Step1Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>An inventory of tables, views, functions, procedures and triggers. What each one reads and writes, what it calls, and which problematic constructs it uses: cursors, dynamic SQL, writes without a transaction.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_Step1Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>1. Static analysis</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_Step2Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>An inexpensive model documents each object separately: what it does in business language, which rules it enforces, and what it should become inside a .NET application.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_Step2Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>2. Interpretation</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_Step3Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A single call to a more capable model, the only one that needs to see the whole graph, orders the migration into phases following the strangler fig pattern: self-contained objects first, hubs last.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_Step3Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>3. Migration plan</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Home_WhatItDoes" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Legacy Lens automates that first step. Give it a script generated with SSMS Generate Scripts and it returns the object inventory, the dependency graph, a justified risk score, documentation for each object in business language, and a phased migration plan.</value>
  </data>
